  <h1>India’s Labour Reforms — Codes, Implementation, and Worker Welfare</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Labour reforms are central to India’s efforts to balance economic growth with worker welfare. In 2024, The Hindu analysed the status of India’s four labour codes, which aim to consolidate 29 laws into a simpler framework. For UPSC, labour reforms highlight the intersection of economy, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Four Codes (2019–20):<br/>o	Code on Wages<br/>o	Industrial Relations Code<br/>o	Social Security Code<br/>o	Occupational Safety, Health & Working Conditions Code<br/>•	Implementation: Delayed, with states yet to finalise rules.<br/>•	Workforce Size: ~500 million workers, of which ~90% are informal.<br/>•	Gig Economy: ~7.5 million gig/platform workers in 2024, set to rise to 25 million by 2030.<br/>•	Unionisation: Weak in unorganised sector.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Reforms<br/>1.	Simplification: Reduced complexity of 29 fragmented laws.<br/>2.	Universal Wage Code: National minimum wage framework.<br/>3.	Social Security: Expanded to gig and platform workers.<br/>4.	Flexibility: Easier hiring and firing norms for businesses.<br/>5.	Worker Protection: Provisions for health, safety, and welfare.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Ease of Business: Simplified compliance boosts investment.<br/>•	Digitalisation: Online labour codes compliance portals.<br/>•	Social Security Expansion: Gig workers formally recognised.<br/>•	Formalisation: Push for EPFO/ESIC coverage in smaller firms.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Delayed Implementation: Codes stuck due to state reluctance.<br/>2.	Informal Sector Exclusion: Many workers unaware of rights.<br/>3.	Worker Insecurity: Easier retrenchment clauses risk exploitation.<br/>4.	Gig Worker Concerns: Social security still not operationalised.<br/>5.	Enforcement Weakness: Labour inspectors understaffed, undertrained.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	e-Shram Portal (2021): Database of 30 crore unorganised workers.<br/>•	Skill India & Apprenticeship Reforms: To align workforce with industry.<br/>•	Labour Welfare Boards: At state level, though underfunded.<br/>•	Digital Monitoring: Shram Suvidha portal for transparency.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: Flexible labour markets attract investment.<br/>•	Social Justice: Expanding security improves worker dignity.<br/>•	Demographic Dividend: Productive workforce central to growth.<br/>•	Global Competitiveness: Reforms align India with global labour standards.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Labour welfare, social justice.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Reforms, ease of doing business.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Labour reforms are about balancing equity with efficiency.”<br/>o	“India’s workers are its strength, not a liability.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Political economy of labour and welfare state.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Implement Codes Quickly: Ensure state–centre coordination.<br/>2.	Protect Workers: Balance employer flexibility with safeguards.<br/>3.	Expand Social Security: Universalise coverage, operationalise gig worker benefits.<br/>4.	Awareness Campaigns: Educate workers on rights and benefits.<br/>5.	Strengthen Inspections: Modernise and digitise enforcement.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Labour reforms are at a critical juncture — they can drive investment and formalisation, but only if paired with strong worker protection. For UPSC aspirants, they highlight the link between economy, governance, and social equity.</p>
